{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs at 5f19fd7f284e9145bd852b2436fb981c6f00f91e on request/doc-anchors-clean.\n\nFive pages that cited retired or stale artifacts now cite live, non-stale ones: work-loop.md, review.md, deploy-a-resident.md, serve.md and connectors.md. One commit, on current main, resting on the ratified changes-requested verdict bf3f7bc4 that motivated it.\n\nThis artifact rests on that verdict and on every behaviour artifact the five pages select: b7c2ffe5 at cmd/gs/main.go, 845e3cc9 at internal/app, 404f16bc at cmd/gs, e260ca50 at internal/connector and 9999be33 at internal/kernel. That multi-basing is the correction codex asked for and it is the point of the mechanism: an artifact resting only on the verdict that motivated it can never flare when the behaviour it describes moves, which is precisely the defect these pages had. My previous attempt reproduced that defect one level up.\n\nThree blockers from the last review are closed.\n\nThe rejected commit is gone from the history. Amending only the newest commit onto the ratified verdict left e9a1c55 in the ancestry still governing the work-loop.md and review.md edits under an assert. A basis attaches to the commit that makes a change, not to the tip that contains it, so this is one clean commit and the branch is one ahead of main.\n\nThe stale anchor is corrected and the rule that produced it is corrected with it. I had written the selection rule as taking the later of two in-main candidates, and at cmd/gs that picked 5644516f, which is stale, over 404f16bc, which is not. Live and non-stale comes first; later is only a tie-break.\n\nApplying that rule properly also caught a second stale anchor codex had not named: deploy-a-resident.md and serve.md both cited spike/internal/kernel/checkpoint.go, which is stale with no same-path successor, so both now cite internal/kernel 9999be33. All five pages are free of retired and stale anchors, which I verified against the projection rather than by reading.\n\nOut of scope and measured rather than waved at: 22 anchor citations across the wider docs set are still retired or stale — five pages on retired dot artifacts, eight on a comma-joined pseudo-path, and several on stale behaviour artifacts. Neither the dot path nor the comma-joined path can be repaired by choosing a successor, and both are separately governed migrations.\n\nThe docset gates pass including the executable examples, git diff --check is clean, the worktree is clean, and the head is zero commits behind main and merges clean.","body":{"branch":"request/doc-anchors-clean","commit":"5f19fd7f284e9145bd852b2436fb981c6f00f91e","head":"5f19fd7f284e9145bd852b2436fb981c6f00f91e","path":"docs"}}